<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What's Your Favorite Food? – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What's Your Favorite Food?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short texts using simple phrases and sentences. Interactive: Respond to written questions from peers.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose short texts for images of food with simple words (e.g., "This is my lunch."). Can respond to written questions from peers about food choices (e.g., "Do you like bananas?" "Yes, I do."). Can write short notes for a lunch order (e.g., "I want a hamburger.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various flashcards of food items (e.g., apple, banana, sandwich, rice with chicken, soup, juice, water). Students look at the pictures. The teacher points to a flashcard and asks, "What's this?" Students say the food item aloud. This quickly activates prior vocabulary knowledge related to food.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher then points to a flashcard (e.g., an apple) and says, "This is an apple. I like apples." The teacher writes "I like apples." on the board, enunciating each word clearly. Then, the teacher points to another flashcard (e.g., soup) and says, "I like soup, but I don't like bananas." The teacher writes "I like soup, but I don't like bananas." on the board. The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /f/ in "favorite" by saying "My favorite food is..." and /s/ in "snack" by saying "I want a snack." The teacher also models blending for /ˈæ-p.əl/ in "apple" and /ˈw-ɔːt-ər/ in "water" while pointing to the written words.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to "I like apples." on the board) "Do I want to eat apples?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to "I like soup, but I don't like bananas.") "Do I eat bananas?" (Expected answer: "No!") "Do I eat soup?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short written text (e.g., a simple lunch order note or a friend's food preferences list). The text includes simple sentences like: "My name is Ana. I like rice with chicken and water for lunch. I want an apple for a snack. I don't like soup. What's your favorite food?" (Genre: short note/list, Register: informal, Format: written text on a worksheet/poster).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking for and giving information about preferences: Expressing likes and dislikes about food (e.g., "I like apples."), and asking about others' food preferences (e.g., "Do you like oranges?").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students receive the written text. The teacher reads it aloud once. Then, students read it silently. The teacher asks simple comprehension questions:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist question: "What is Ana talking about?" (Expected: food/lunch).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: Students circle 'T' or 'F' next to sentences based on the text (e.g., "Ana likes soup. T/F", "Ana wants an apple for a snack. T/F").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information matching: Students match words from the text to simple pictures (e.g., 'rice with chicken' to a picture of it).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing WH- and yes/no questions, using present simple sentences to express likes/dislikes, and compound sentences with 'but'. They will use target vocabulary (apple, banana, sandwich, lunch, breakfast, dinner, food, snack, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup, eat, drink, taste, choose, want, enjoy, favorite, tasty, healthy).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-blanks):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences that have blanks. Students fill in the blanks with appropriate target vocabulary words or grammar structures. Example sentences: "I ____ rice with chicken." (like/want) "My favorite ____ is banana." (food) "I want ____ water." (some) "Do you ____ soup?" (like) "I like apples, ____ I don't like bananas." (but).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Builder):</b> Students are given sentence frames and a choice of words. They write full sentences by selecting and inserting words. For example: "I want a ______." (choices: apple, sandwich, juice) Students write three sentences: "I want an apple.", "I want a sandwich.", "I want juice." Another example: "Do you like ______?" (choices: soup, banana, rice) Students write three questions: "Do you like soup?", "Do you like banana?", "Do you like rice?".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Written Q&A):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes three simple 'Yes/No' questions about food preferences on a small paper strip (e.g., "Do you like apples?", "Do you like soup?", "Do you want juice?"). They exchange strips with their partner and write a 'Yes, I do.' or 'No, I don't.' response below each question. They then return the strips to check each other's answers.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written product – "My Lunch Order" or "My Favorite Food Note".</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher gives each student a worksheet titled "My Lunch Order!" or "What I Eat!". The worksheet has spaces for students to write short sentences about what they want to eat for lunch, their favorite food, and what they don't like. There can be small picture prompts to help them. Students are instructed to:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write what they want for lunch (e.g., "I want rice with chicken and water.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write their favorite food (e.g., "My favorite food is sandwich.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write one food they don't like, using 'but' (e.g., "I like bananas, but I don't like soup.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Optionally, they can draw a picture next to their writing.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After individual writing, students can discreetly exchange their notes with one or two peers. Each peer writes one yes/no question in the margin (e.g., "Do you like water?"). The original writer then responds to the written question with "Yes, I do." or "No, I don't." beneath the question. This allows for interactive written communication.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the writing activities and collection of the "My Lunch Order!" notes for a quick check. A simple rubric can be used for evaluation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' completed "My Lunch Order!" or "My Favorite Food Note" worksheet and their written responses to peer questions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes simple sentences (S-V-O structure).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (food nouns, 'want', 'like', 'don't like').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Composes short texts for images/preferences.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds accurately to written Yes/No questions from peers.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use 'but' for compound sentences.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written message (understandable to peers/teacher).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small paper slip:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food words did you write today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'I like bananas'?" (Students check a box for Yes/No).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write about food today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was a little difficult for you when writing?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The writing activities worked well in integrating the target vocabulary and grammar. Students were generally able to compose simple sentences and respond to peer questions. Some students struggled with the spelling of new words, requiring more visual aids or a word bank. The 'but' conjunction proved challenging for a few, needing more explicit examples and guided practice. I will ensure to provide a clear word bank for the next writing task and possibly introduce a "word wall" with common food vocabulary.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their food preferences and orders in a collaborative project, possibly presenting their 'menu' or 'favorite food collage' to the class, which will consolidate their writing and speaking skills further. They will use the written notes from this lesson as a basis for their final output.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>